<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Effectiveness on off-the-shelf Foundation Models</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further investigate whether our method can effectively enhance existing, large-scale foundation models. To this end, we take the official LLaMA-3-3B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite> model and perform continual pre-training on 5 billion tokens from the SlimPajama dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
We compare the original model’s performance with two versions after this additional training: a vanilla continual pretraining baseline and our proposed approach. As illustrated in <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 4.4 Effectiveness on off-the-shelf Foundation Models ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Pretraining LLM with Latent Thoughtsin Continuous Space" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 8</span></a>, our method achieves a lower training loss than the vanilla baseline after consuming less than 1B tokens, and this performance gap widens as training progresses. As shown in <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.4 Effectiveness on off-the-shelf Foundation Models ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Pretraining LLM with Latent Thoughtsin Continuous Space" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a>, evaluation on our nine standard downstream tasks reveals that our method provides a substantial boost in performance, demonstrating its utility as a plug-and-play enhancement for off-the-shelf models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F8.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:190.8pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.23184v1/train_curves.svg" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:264/188;" width="264" height="188"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Training loss comparison of our method and vanilla continual pretraining for the LLaMA-3-3B on 5B tokens from SlimPajama.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F8.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:190.8pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.23184v1/scaling_ablation_combined.svg" id="S4.F8.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:264/189;" width="264" height="189"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>(Top) Impact of the num of Jacobi iterations .
(Bottom) Chaining more latent thoughts before each token consistently lowers loss.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
